 I have an array of objects that I want to sort based on a string property. I want the sorting to be case-insensitive. How can I achieve this in JavaScript?

A:

You can use the `localeCompare` method with the `sensitivity: 'base'` option to perform case-insensitive string comparison. Here's an example:

```javascript
const arr = [
  { name: 'Alice' },
  { name: 'bob' },
  { name: 'Charlie' },
  { name: 'david' }
];

arr.sort((a, b) => a.name.localeCompare(b.name, undefined, { sensitivity: 'base' }));

console.log(arr);
// Output: [{ name: 'Alice' }, { name: 'bob' }, { name: 'Charlie' }, { name: 'david' }]
```

In this example, the `localeCompare` method is used to compare the `name` properties of the objects. The third argument is an options object with `sensitivity: 'base'`, which makes the comparison case-insensitive.

Alternatively, you can convert both strings to lowercase before comparing:

```javascript
arr.sort((a, b) => a.name.toLowerCase().localeCompare(b.name.toLowerCase()));
```

Both approaches will produce the same result. The `localeCompare` method with `sensitivity: 'base'` is generally more robust because it handles Unicode characters correctly and follows the user's locale settings.

